1. `common_elements(a, b)`: The initial implementation might involve nested loops to compare each element of `a` with each element of `b`. This results in O(n*m) time complexity where n and m are the lengths of lists a and b.  A more efficient solution is using sets. Convert both lists to sets, find their intersection, then convert the result back to a sorted list. This approach should be close to O(n + m), which will likely meet the 2-second budget for two 200,000 element lists.

2. `fib(n)`: The naive recursive implementation has exponential time complexity. A dynamic programming approach (memoization) or an iterative solution can reduce this to linear time complexity O(n).  I'll implement the iterative version which is generally more efficient than memoization in Python.

3. `count_pairs(nums, target)`: Similar to `common_elements`, a naive implementation using nested loops would be O(n^2). Using a hash map (dictionary) can achieve O(n) time complexity by storing the required values and their frequencies. This should easily pass the 2-second test.

4. `dedupe_keep_order(items)`:  Using a set to track seen items is an efficient way to remove duplicates while preserving order. Iterating through the input list and adding each item to the set if it's not already present, will give an O(n) time complexity where n is the length of the list.

